nt). Static scan, not a runtime canary: the c372 damage came from a MID-EDIT test file, exactly the state a canary misses -- the scan reads the bytes the next suite run loads. Negative test: the scanner flags a planted unbound git op (guard that cannot fail is not a guard, c362). Guard-authoring law applied to the guard itself: scanners strip comment-only lines -- this file's own discussion of the bug shapes fired on itself on first run (c362's scar reproduced and fixed same-hour). Suite 1302 green (1300 + 2).\n6a0ed3b c368 belt #2d: refusal branch heals at the action site (annotate + retry once with audited escape + un-stage on failure).\ndd9572f c367 belt #2c: the belt now PUSHES its commit (durable -> visible).\ncb6ea45 c364 belt #2 fix: hook REFUSAL no longer conflated with nothing-to-commit (exit 1). Production: continuo 2026-09-15 10:35Z -- HISTORY-CLOCK guard refused her belt commit (fabricated timestamp), belt read exit 1 as durable-success, record stayed undurable + refused blob sat staged in shared index. Belt now captures commit stderr+stdout, matches REFUSED, returns nil (honest not-durable). Test: refusal-honest-nil (refusing-hook repo). Suite 1293 green.\n09bf172 fix: belt2b TZ boundary test - use variables for today/yesterday to avoid hard-coded dates\n\n[t+06:43/60:00 c83/300]"}]
